  "dossiers": [
    {
      "description": "Nationalism can name attachments, programs, or movements concerning nations and self-determination; nation and state remain distinct, and asserted unity cannot establish inclusion or practice.",
      "id": "nationalism-dossier",
      "kind": "dossier",
      "label": "Nationalism dossier",
      "publicationStatus": "reviewed",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-09-06",
      "sections": [
        {
          "body": "The Stanford account separates national identity from action for self-determination and distinguishes nations from states.
Anderson instead emphasizes how people imagine a limited and sovereign political community, while Chatterjee asks how anticolonial nationalism remained constrained by categories it opposed.
Tagore's critique targets an organized political and commercial Nation rather than treating every form of collective belonging as nationalism.",
          "heading": "Which meanings must stay separate?",
          "id": "meanings",
          "statementIds": [
            "nationalism-attitude-program-boundary",
            "nation-state-boundary",
            "anderson-imagined-community",
            "chatterjee-derivative-dispute",
            "tagore-nation-society-rival",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Self-determination may be pursued through an independent state, autonomy, federation, or other arrangements.
Formal sovereignty and effective control are also separate: having a state does not prove that a community determines its important choices.",
          "heading": "Must self-determination mean statehood?",
          "id": "statuses",
          "statementIds": [
            "self-determination-statehood-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "National fraternity can be asserted amid unequal power and exclusion.
Race, caste, class, gender, religion, language, region, citizenship, and Indigenous status require their own evidence; unity or self-description cannot answer who participated or benefited.",
          "heading": "Who belongs to the asserted nation?",
          "id": "boundaries",
          "statementIds": [
            "anderson-horizontal-inequality",
            "chatterjee-state-unity-limit",
            "nationalism-self-description-limit",
            "national-unity-exclusion-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "In 1897, Hui Aloha ʻĀina collected 21,269 Native Hawaiian signatures on a bilingual petition opposing annexation.
The campaign helped defeat a treaty, but Congress annexed Hawaiʻi by joint resolution in 1898.
At Bandung in 1955, twenty-nine governments joined anticolonial principles to sovereignty and non-intervention; Ghana's 1957 legal independence supplies a distinct status change.
These episodes show different national, Indigenous, anticolonial, and international claims, not one sequence or definition.",
          "heading": "What do Hawaiʻi, Bandung, and Ghana show?",
          "id": "bounded-practice",
          "relatedEntityRefs": [
            {
              "id": "hawaiian-overthrow-annexation-1893-1898",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "bandung-conference-1955",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "ghana-independence-1957",
              "kind": "case",
            },
          ],
          "statementIds": [
            "hawaii-petition-scale",
            "hawaii-annexation-sequence",
            "bandung-participation",
            "bandung-anticolonial-principles",
            "ghana-legal-independence",
            "ghana-worldmaking-project",
            "decolonization-sequence-limit",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Compare who claims national membership, which status they seek, which organization speaks, and which rules operate.
Patriotism, citizenship, anticolonial politics, and Indigenous nationhood may overlap nationalism, but none can be inferred from another.",
          "heading": "What should comparison keep separate?",
          "id": "comparisons",
          "statementIds": [
            "nationalism-attitude-program-boundary",
            "self-determination-statehood-boundary",
            "nationalism-self-description-limit",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
      ],
      "standfirst": "Nationalism can describe attachments to a nation, programs for political authority, or movements pursuing self-determination.
It does not make nation, state, country, people, ethnicity, citizenship, and homeland synonyms, and a nationalist label does not establish institutions or inclusion in practice.",
      "standfirstStatementIds": [
        "nationalism-attitude-program-boundary",
        "nation-state-boundary",
        "nationalism-self-description-limit",
      ],
      "subject": {
        "id": "nationalism",
        "kind": "concept",
      },
    },
    {
      "description": "Colonialism concerns subjugation rather than one universal stage; settlement, indirect command, political concepts, and community political language require separate evidence.",
      "id": "colonialism-dossier",
      "kind": "dossier",
      "label": "Colonialism dossier",
      "publicationStatus": "reviewed",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-09-06",
      "sections": [
        {
          "body": "Kohn and Reddy define colonialism through subjugation while acknowledging its disputed boundary with imperialism.
Colonization can name settlement or a process of establishing colonies, whereas colonialism asks about a sustained relation of domination.
Césaire rejects the claimed equation between colonization and civilization; Cooper warns that an all-purpose category can hide specific institutions and political alternatives.",
          "heading": "How do colonialism and colonization differ?",
          "id": "meanings",
          "statementIds": [
            "colonialism-domination-definition",
            "colonization-colonialism-boundary",
            "cesaire-colonization-civilization-rival",
            "cooper-category-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Cooper's publisher summary emphasizes political mobilizations that reconfigured citizenship and equality in colonial settings.
Duke's description of Silva's work instead emphasizes evidence recovered from Hawaiian-language newspapers and anti-annexation petitions.",
          "heading": "Which arrangements changed land, labor, and rule?",
          "id": "arrangements",
          "statementIds": [
            "colonial-arrangements-plural",
            "colonial-formal-practice-boundary",
            "colonial-archive-mediation",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Getachew argues that racialized inequality among formally sovereign states prompted projects for regional federation and transformed international institutions.
Whether particular property, labor, administrative, military, or financial relations persisted after Ghanaian independence remains an open research question here.",
          "heading": "Does independence end colonial institutions?",
          "id": "decolonization",
          "statementIds": [
            "decolonization-independence-boundary",
            "ghana-neocolonial-limit",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Silva's account names three Hawaiian political organizations in the anti-annexation coalition and preserves James Keauiluna Kaulia's use of *ka lāhui* for the opposing public.
This Hawaiian-language political claim is evidence in its own right, not an English category supplied by the project.",
          "heading": "Whose names and evidence define the boundary?",
          "id": "boundaries",
          "statementIds": [
            "kaulia-lahui-annexation-refusal",
            "silva-continuing-sovereignty-claim",
            "hawaii-petition-language-provenance",
            "colonial-modernization-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "The Hawaiian overthrow-and-annexation episode documents organized resistance as well as an imposed change of sovereignty.
Ghana's 1957 statute documents formal independence, while later external economic constraint remains a separate proposition requiring separate evidence.
Tawantinsuyu supplies a cross-period comparison of imperial institutions, not an early colony, modern nation-state, or stage on a universal path.",
          "heading": "What can bounded transitions establish?",
          "id": "bounded-practice",
          "relatedEntityRefs": [
            {
              "id": "hawaiian-overthrow-annexation-1893-1898",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "ghana-independence-1957",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "tawantinsuyu-imperial-organization",
              "kind": "case",
            },
          ],
          "statementIds": [
            "hawaii-case-boundary",
            "ghana-legal-independence",
            "ghana-neocolonial-limit",
            "decolonization-sequence-limit",
            "tawantinsuyu-non-embodiment",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Compare settlement with indirect command, political concepts with the institutions using them, and independence with changes in specific institutions.
Cooper's rejection of an inevitable empire-to-nation sequence is a warning against turning these categories into universal stages.",
          "heading": "How should colonial institutions be compared?",
          "id": "comparisons",
          "statementIds": [
            "colonial-formal-practice-boundary",
            "colonial-archive-mediation",
            "decolonization-independence-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
      ],
      "standfirst": "Colonialism names practices in which one people subjugates another.
Its boundary with colonization and imperialism is disputed, so settlement and indirect command must not be collapsed into one institutional form.",
      "standfirstStatementIds": [
        "colonialism-domination-definition",
        "colonial-arrangements-plural",
        "colonial-modernization-boundary",
      ],
      "subject": {
        "id": "colonialism",
        "kind": "concept",
      },
    },
    {
      "description": "Imperialism can name extended-power practices or theories, not every empire or unequal relationship; formal status and attributed mechanisms remain separate.",
      "id": "imperialism-dossier",
      "kind": "dossier",
      "label": "Imperialism dossier",
      "publicationStatus": "reviewed",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-09-06",
      "sections": [
        {
          "body": "Kohn and Reddy emphasize command through settlement, sovereignty, or indirect control.
Nkrumah instead argues that formally independent states can remain directed from outside through economic and policy mechanisms.
These definitions overlap but ask for different evidence and cannot be converted into a timeless country label.",
          "heading": "How does imperialism differ from empire?",
          "id": "meanings",
          "statementIds": [
            "imperialism-power-extension-definition",
            "empire-imperialism-boundary",
            "sep-indirect-imperial-boundary",
            "nkrumah-neocolonial-definition",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "In Nkrumah's account, external control can survive independence through security footholds, privileges over territory, resources, and government functions, or the conditions attached to aid.
This is his diagnosis; applying it elsewhere requires evidence that a specified privilege operated in that relationship.",
          "heading": "How can power extend beyond formal sovereignty?",
          "id": "mechanisms",
          "statementIds": [
            "imperial-mechanisms-plural",
            "formal-informal-empire-boundary",
            "imperial-allegation-proof-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Capitalist theories connect imperial expansion to accumulation, while geopolitical, strategic, cultural, and state-centered explanations make rival predictions.
Civilizing language or cultural influence may belong to an imperial project, but rhetoric alone does not establish who could compel whom.",
          "heading": "Which explanations compete?",
          "id": "theories",
          "statementIds": [
            "capitalist-imperialism-rival-boundary",
            "imperial-cultural-project-boundary",
            "nkrumah-neocolonial-definition",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "The Hawaiian annexation sequence shows direct territorial extension despite mass recorded opposition.
Bandung's communiqué opposed colonialism while defending sovereign equality, and Getachew reads related projects as attempts to remake international order rather than stop at isolated nation-states.
Nkrumah's later neo-colonial diagnosis makes Ghanaian legal independence and continuing economic autonomy separate research questions.",
          "heading": "What do annexation and anticolonial worldmaking show?",
          "id": "bounded-practice",
          "relatedEntityRefs": [
            {
              "id": "hawaiian-overthrow-annexation-1893-1898",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "bandung-conference-1955",
              "kind": "case",
            },
            {
              "id": "ghana-independence-1957",
              "kind": "case",
            },
          ],
          "statementIds": [
            "hawaii-annexation-sequence",
            "bandung-anticolonial-principles",
            "bandung-worldmaking-boundary",
            "ghana-neocolonial-limit",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Ask whether the evidence concerns an empire's institutions, an actor's imperial program, a cross-border relation, or an attributed theory explaining it.
Cooper and Getachew both reject an inevitable passage from empire to isolated nation-states, while addressing different histories and political alternatives.",
          "heading": "What evidence would establish imperial practice?",
          "id": "comparisons",
          "statementIds": [
            "empire-imperialism-boundary",
            "imperial-allegation-proof-boundary",
            "imperial-country-essence-boundary",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
      ],
      "standfirst": "Imperialism can name a project, practice, relation, or explanatory theory for extending power beyond an existing political center.
It is not the same thing as a historically bounded empire, every unequal relationship, or an accusation detached from a demonstrated mechanism.",
      "standfirstStatementIds": [
        "imperialism-power-extension-definition",
        "empire-imperialism-boundary",
        "imperial-allegation-proof-boundary",
      ],
      "subject": {
        "id": "imperialism",
        "kind": "concept",
      },
    },
  ],
